# ESP32-C5 only: ESP-IDF loads this after sdkconfig.defaults for IDF_TARGET
# esp32c5, so these lines override the shared 16 KiB, internal-RAM TCP
# configuration for the C5 DevKitC-1 and nothing else.
#
# lwIP pbufs and TCP segments, and the Wi-Fi driver's heap allocations
# (including its dynamic RX buffers), prefer the 8 MB PSRAM, so TCP windows
# stop costing scarce internal RAM. That pays for 64 KiB windows in each
# direction. Wi-Fi DMA buffers stay in internal RAM.
#
# A 64 KiB receive window needs a receive path deep enough to take a whole
# window in one burst: with ESP-IDF's 32 dynamic RX buffers and 4 queued
# out-of-order segments, a paired gadget lost segments about ten times per
# 4 MB transfer and VM-to-gadget throughput fell to 1.8 Mbit/s. 64 dynamic RX
# buffers and 12 out-of-order segments fix it (tools/coexist/README.md has
# the numbers). The block-ack window stays at sdkconfig.defaults' 32.
#
# PSRAM can fail to start (CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND keeps the gadget
# running): lwIP and Wi-Fi then fall back to internal RAM. The out-of-order
# queue stays bounded (12 segments per connection, not ESP-IDF's "0 =
# unlimited") so even then a connection can't queue a whole window there, and
# the gadget logs the missing PSRAM at boot.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_TRY_ALLOCATE_WIFI_LWIP=y
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_SND_BUF_DEFAULT=65535
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_WND_DEFAULT=65535
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_DYNAMIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM=64
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_OOSEQ_MAX_PBUFS=12
